--TEST--
`Core\Sse->receive` asks about the door and never about the handle, so the one `stream()` answered and the one `current()` answers are refused in the same words — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The handle carries nothing: what decides whether there is a wait to be had is
// the context this program is, not which member built the object it was called
// on. A program that could reach a waiting stream by asking for the handle a
// second way would have two answers to one question.
var $opened = Core\Sse::stream();
try {
    $opened->receive();
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Sse::current()->receive();
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
`Core\Sse::receive` needs a connection and this program is a streaming response: a request answering with its own events ends when they do, so a publisher has nothing to reach it on — only a script `Core\Sse::upgrade` opened can wait
`Core\Sse::receive` needs a connection and this program is a streaming response: a request answering with its own events ends when they do, so a publisher has nothing to reach it on — only a script `Core\Sse::upgrade` opened can wait
